@weswigham I do not believe your assertion that this is the same as ESM is true as @jkrems has pointed out more specifically than I. Either way, the framing continues to be problematic in light of concerns that multiple people have spoken about.

per writing tests, add them to the `test/es-module` directory is the likely best location and use the `common` helpers for various tasks. see [docs](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/63f5a76c1dd9e9c3d4851d41eb1593d9838bad71/doc/guides/writing-tests.md) for more details.

my particular concern is about testing the alteration in when events are allowed to occur and I want explicit tests about how this does not work like ESM historically has/TLA works.

I would like to ask why it's important `require` can load TLA modules. At current people kinda depend on `require(pseudoEsm)` via tooling and stuff, however as written all of those modules are synchronous given tooling mostly still doesn't support TLA.

With that in mind is it really necessary to support TLA in `require(esm)` rather than just rejecting modules containing it? If this is primarily about ensuring existing authored code can work with the new module system then it would be sufficient to just allow `require(syncEsm)` and throw an error if TLA is encountered.

e.g.:

```js
function require(path) {
  // ...
  if (module.isTransitivelyAsync) {
    throw new Error("sync require of module containing TLA is not supported");
  }
  module.evaluate();
  return module.namespace;
}
```

Or perhaps even just give a warning and return the uninstantiated namespace:

```js
if (module.isTransitivelyAsync) {
  console.warning("[warning] sync require of module containing TLA, namespace in TDZ returned");
}
// ...
```

> that would make more moving to TLA a breaking change, which would be bad. it's currently an implementation detail of a module.

I've [mentioned this](https://github.com/nodejs/modules/issues/388#issuecomment-536444025) in the past, in general changing sync->async is a breaking change for consumers that can't already handle Promises.

For example suppose I'm doing this:

```js
const globber = new glob.Glob('*');

globber.on('match', file => {
  console.log(`Before read: ${ file }`);
  const contents = fs.readFileSync(file);
  console.log(`After read: ${ file }`);
});
```

With this code there is an invariant that if the files in order are `a`, `b` and `c` that it'll print:

```
Before read: a
After read: a
Before read: b
After read: b
Before read: c
After read: c
```

However I can't just change it to async and expect the invariants to hold:

```js
const globber = new glob.Glob('*');

globber.on('match', async file => {
  console.log(`Before read: ${ file }`);
  const contents = await fs.promises.readFile(file);
  console.log(`After read: ${ file }`);
});
```

This is because Node's `EventEmitter` does not `await` the previous event callback before proceeding to the next one.

TLDR: If the library calls a consumer's functions synchronously then the consumer of that library cannot rely on timing invariants if they upgrade to `async`/`Promise`.

And quoting myself:

> This is a [large point of contention](https://journal.stuffwithstuff.com/2015/02/01/what-color-is-your-function/) for async/await in general, basically transforming to async generally implies some friction on one side. However I don't think this has significantly hurt the adoption of Promises despite many libraries (and builtins on the web especially) not even offering synchronous variants, hence forcing their consumers to upgrade to Promises.

My feelings are strongly that if TLA/WASM modules are actually popular, then libraries that need to support the feature will upgrade to `import()`.

I think `require(esm)` is only needed as a stopgap so that users of libraries which will never be updated to use `import()` can continue to be used for a large subset of ESM without fundamentally breaking language invariants.

@Jamesernator There's an important difference between TLA and Promise-returning functions: From a JS-code perspective, TLA doesn't change how a function works. You example for making a *function* async is correct - that's a breaking change. Using TLA isn't though because the observable interface of the module doesn't become async. The code in your example couldn't be written to break without involving cross-file execution timing being important. Which already isn't reliable before TLA, so that's not a change.

```js
// This code isn't safe! There's no guarantee that create-emitter hasn't been imported before
// so it may already have run. Nothing ensures that the emitter will be created sync.
import './event-emitter-singleton';

// You don't need this line to make the code unsafe.
// import './tla-code';

// Might miss events if event-emitter-singleton has already been imported in the past
import './attach-handler-to-emitter-singleton';
```

> My feelings are strongly that if TLA/WASM modules are actually popular, then libraries that need to support the feature will upgrade to `import()`.

The problem isn't as much that an immediate dependency A adds TLA/WASM. The problem is that an ESM library B ten layers down wants to use WASM to speed up some small bit of code and breaks a CJS consumer ten layers of indirection up. Or a CJS library 5 layers down (with all sync APIs in each library along the way) uses `require(esm)` and runs into the previous ESM dep B change 5 more layers into the dep tree.

Without `require(esm)`, the way that `import` statements work make it perfectly safe to have TLA in the dependency tree. With the restriction above about patterns that weren't safe in the first place.

